text: "---\nmodule: \"03-SALES-CLOSING\"\npart: \"D\"\ntitle: \"Negotiation Playbook\"\nversion: \"2026.05\"\nstatus: \"Production-Ready\"\n---\n\n# 03D: Negotiation Playbook\n\n> **Core Principle:** Negotiation is not about winning. It is about finding the overlap between what they need and what you can deliver profitably.\n>\n> *\"Be confident even if you are nervous and screaming inside. Sell results, not your service.\"* — Him Singh\n\n---\n\n## Table of Contents\n\n1. [The Negotiation Mindset](#1-the-negotiation-mindset)\n2. [Objection Response Library](#2-objection-response-library)\n3. [Scope Creep Defense](#3-scope-creep-defense)\n4. [The Art of the Upsell](#4-the-art-of-the-upsell)\n5. [Closing Techniques](#5-closing-techniques)\n6. [AI Prompts](#6-ai-prompts)\n7. [Action Checklist](#7-action-checklist)\n\n---\n\n## 1. The Negotiation Mindset\n\n### The BATNA Framework\n\n| Element | Your Position | Their Position |\n|---------|---------------|----------------|\n| **Best Alternative** | Other clients, savings, different projects | Other freelancers, internal team, delay |\n| **Walk-Away Price** | Minimum profitable rate | Maximum budget |\n| **Target** | Value-based price | Fair market rate |\n| **Aspiration** | Premium pricing | Best value |\n\n### The 3 Rules of Freelance Negotiation\n\n| Rule | Application |\n|------|-------------|\n| **Never negotiate against yourself** | Don't lower price before they object |\n| **Trade, don't concede** | Every concession gets a counter-request |\n| **Silence is power** | After stating price, stop talking |\n\n### Pre-Negotiation Preparation\n\nBefore every negotiation, know:\n- [ ] Your walk-away price (minimum acceptable)\n- [ ] Your target price (ideal outcome)\n- [ ] Your best alternative (what happens if no deal)\n- [ ] Their likely budget (from discovery call)\n- [ ] Their alternatives (other freelancers, internal team)\n- [ ] 3 value points that justify your price\n\n---\n\n## 2. Objection Response Library\n\n### The 8 Most Common Objections\n\n#### 1. \"Your price is too high\"\n\n**Don't say:** \"I can lower it\"  \n**Do say:**\n```\n\"What were you expecting to invest? And what scope were you \nimagining at that price?\"\n```\n\n**Then:**\n```\n\"I can adjust the scope to fit your budget. Which deliverables \nare must-haves vs. nice-to-haves?\"\n```\n\n**Psychology:** Reframe from \"expensive\" to \"adjustable scope\"\n\n---\n\n#### 2. \"Your competitor charges less\"\n\n**Don't say:** \"But I'm better\"  \n**Do say:**\n```\n\"What does their scope include? Many freelancers quote lower \nprices by excluding deployment, documentation, or monitoring. \n\nMy price includes [specific inclusions], which ensures the system \nworks after I leave.\"\n```\n\n**Psychology:** Compare value, not price\n\n---\n\n#### 3. \"We don't have the budget right now\"\n\n**Don't say:** \"When will you?\"  \n**Do say:**\n```\n\"What's the cost of not solving this in the next 3 months? \nIf [pain] continues at [rate], that's [quantified loss].\n\nWe could start with the Starter tier ($1,500) to build the \nroadmap, then execute the full project next quarter.\"\n```\n\n**Psychology:** Cost of inaction > cost of action\n\n---\n\n#### 4. \"We need to think about it\"\n\n**Don't say:** \"No problem, take your time\"  \n**Do say:**\n```\n\"Totally understand. What specific concerns do you have? \nIs it the scope, timeline, or investment?\"\n```\n\n**Then:**\n```\n\"Would it help if I [specific action: break into phases, \nadjust timeline, provide references]?\"\n```\n\n**Psychology:** Surface the real objection\n\n---\n\n#### 5. \"Can you do a small test first?\"\n\n**Don't say:** \"Sure, free trial\"  \n**Do say:**\n```\n\"I offer a paid pilot for $[Amount] that includes [specific \nscope]. This lets us validate the approach with real data \nbefore the full investment.\n\nIf we proceed to the full project, the pilot fee is credited \ntoward the total.\"\n```\n\n**Psychology:** Paid pilots filter serious clients\n\n---\n\n#### 6. \"We want to own all the IP\"\n\n**Don't say:** \"Okay, no problem\"  \n**Do say:**\n```\n\"You own all final deliverables upon full payment. I retain \nrights to general methodologies and pre-existing tools I've \ndeveloped across projects.\n\nThis protects both of us — you get full ownership of what \nyou paid for, and I can continue improving my frameworks.\"\n```\n\n**Psychology:** Fair IP split, not total surrender\n\n---\n\n#### 7. \"We need to run this by [boss/board]\"\n\n**Don't say:** \"Sure, let me know\"  \n**Do say:**\n```\n\"Absolutely. What information would be most helpful for them? \nI can prepare a one-page summary of scope, timeline, and ROI \nthat you can forward.\n\nWould it help if I joined the next meeting to answer technical \nquestions?\"\n```\n\n**Psychology:** Make them the hero, equip them to sell internally\n\n---\n\n#### 8. \"Can you start immediately?\"\n\n**Don't say:** \"Yes, right away\" (unless true)  \n**Do say:**\n```\n\"I can begin discovery and planning immediately, with full \ndevelopment starting [date]. This actually benefits you — \nI'll use the prep time to research [specific preparation] \nso we hit the ground running.\n\nRushed projects often require rework. Let's do this right.\"\n```\n\n**Psychology:** Professional boundaries increase perceived value\n\n---\n\n## 3. Scope Creep Defense\n\n### The Change Request Process\n\n```\nClient asks for extra work\n        ↓\n\"Great idea. Let me assess the impact on timeline and budget.\"\n        ↓\n[24-hour pause — never agree immediately]\n        ↓\n\"This adds [X] hours and [Y] days. I can do it for $[Z] \nor we can add it to Phase 2. Which works better?\"\n        ↓\n[They choose: pay more, delay, or drop]\n        ↓\nDocument in writing (email or change order)\n        ↓\nProceed only after written confirmation\n```\n\n### The Scope Creep Phrases\n\n| When They Say... | You Say... |\n|------------------|------------|\n| \"Can you just add...?\" | \"Let me check the impact on timeline and budget\" |\n| \"It won't take long\" | \"You're probably right, but let me confirm so we both have clarity\" |\n| \"While you're at it...\" | \"Happy to — I'll add it to the change request\" |\n| \"The old freelancer did it\" | \"I scope projects differently to ensure quality. Let me quote this separately\" |\n| \"It's a small change\" | \"Small changes add up. Let's track them so you have visibility\" |\n\n### The Change Order Template\n\n```\nSubject: Change Request — [Project Name]\n\nHi [Name],\n\nPer your request to add [new scope], I've assessed the impact:\n\n**Original SOW:** [Brief description]\n**Requested Change:** [New item]\n**Impact on Timeline:** [+X days/weeks]\n**Impact on Budget:** [+$Amount]\n**Updated Total:** [$New Amount]\n\nOptions:\n1. Approve change order for $[Amount] and [New Timeline]\n2. Defer to Phase 2 (separate SOW after current project)\n3. Adjust other scope to accommodate (which scope to reduce?)\n\nPlease reply with your preference and I'll update the SOW.\n\nBest,\n[Your name]\n```\n\n---\n\n## 4. The Art of the Upsell\n\n### Natural Upsell Moments\n\n| Moment | Upsell Opportunity | Script |\n|--------|---------------------|--------|\n| **During discovery** | \"While we're at it...\" | \"Since I'm already building the pipeline, adding [feature] would only add [X] hours but give you [Y benefit]\" |\n| **At delivery** | \"Now that this is live...\" | \"This is working great. Have you considered [next capability]?\" |\n| **At 30-day check** | \"Looking at the data...\" | \"The model is performing well. If we added [enhancement], I estimate [X% improvement]\" |\n| **Quarterly review** | \"For next quarter...\" | \"Based on your roadmap, [new project] would be a natural next step\" |\n\n### The Upsell Framework\n\n```\n1. ACKNOWLEDGE current success\n   \"The churn model is predicting with 89% accuracy...\"\n\n2. IDENTIFY adjacent opportunity\n   \"...and I noticed your LTV data is clean enough to add \n    a customer segmentation model\"\n\n3. QUANTIFY the added value\n   \"This would let you personalize onboarding, which typically \n    improves activation by 15-20%\"\n\n4. MAKE IT EASY\n   \"Since the data pipeline is already built, this would only \n    take 2 weeks and $3,000\"\n\n5. ASK FOR THE NEXT STEP\n   \"Worth exploring in our next check-in?\"\n```\n\n---\n\n## 5. Closing Techniques\n\n### The Assumptive Close\n\n```\n\"Great. I'll send the SOW and invoice for the deposit today. \nOnce that's signed and paid, we can kick off Monday. \nDoes that timeline work for you?\"\n```\n\n**When to use:** They've expressed enthusiasm, no major objections\n\n### The Alternative Close\n\n```\n\"Would you prefer to start with the Starter audit, or go \nstraight to the full MVP build?\"\n```\n\n**When to use:** They're interested but undecided on scope\n\n### The Urgency Close\n\n```\n\"I have availability starting [date], but my next opening \nafter that is [later date]. Would you like to lock in \nthe [earlier date]?\"\n```\n\n**When to use:** Genuine scarcity (don't fake it)\n\n### The Summary Close\n\n```\n\"Let me make sure I have this right: You need [deliverable], \ndelivered by [date], with [specific requirement]. The \ninvestment is $[Amount], with 50% to start. \n\nIf that all sounds right, I'll send the paperwork now. \nSound good?\"\n```\n\n**When to use:** Complex projects, multiple stakeholders\n\n### The \"Reply YES\" Close [Him Singh Style]\n\n```\n\"Kindly reply 'YES' to confirm and agree. If you have any \nquestions or want to change something, feel free to ask.\"\n```\n\n**When to use:** Email-based agreements, smaller projects\n\n---\n\n## 6. AI Prompts\n\n### Prompt 1: Objection Response Trainer\n\n```\nAct as a negotiation coach.\n\nI just received this objection from a prospect:\n\"[PASTE OBJECTION]\"\n\nContext:\n- My price: $[Amount]\n- Their budget hint: $[Range]\n- Project scope: [DESCRIPTION]\n- My experience: [YEARS + RESULTS]\n\nWrite 3 response options:\n1. Direct and confident (for when you have leverage)\n2. Collaborative and flexible (for when you want the relationship)\n3. Boundary-setting (for when they're being unreasonable)\n\nEach response must:\n- Be under 75 words\n- Include one question\n- Move the conversation forward\n- Protect your value\n```\n\n### Prompt 2: Scope Creep Defender\n\n```\nAct as a scope management consultant.\n\nCurrent project:\n- SOW scope: [WHAT'S INCLUDED]\n- Price: $[Amount]\n- Timeline: [WEEKS]\n\nClient just asked for:\n\"[PASTE REQUEST]\"\n\nDraft a response that:\n1. Validates their request (not defensive)\n2. Explains impact on timeline\n3. Explains impact on budget\n4. Offers 2-3 alternatives\n5. Asks for written confirmation\n\nTone: Collaborative, professional, firm on boundaries.\n```\n\n### Prompt 3: Upsell Opportunity Finder\n\n```\nAct as a growth strategist for freelance services.\n\nCurrent client project:\n- What we built: [DESCRIPTION]\n- Client's business: [TYPE]\n- Their current metrics: [NUMBERS]\n- Their stated goals: [OBJECTIVES]\n\nIdentify 3 natural upsell opportunities:\n1. Immediate (can add to current project)\n2. Near-term (30-60 days post-launch)\n3. Strategic (next quarter)\n\nFor each:\n- The opportunity\n- Why it fits their goals\n- Estimated value to them\n- Your price\n- How to introduce it\n```\n\n---\n\n## 7. Action Checklist\n\n### Before Every Negotiation\n\n- [ ] Review discovery call notes (quantified pain)\n- [ ] Confirm your walk-away price\n- [ ] Prepare 3 value justifications\n- [ ] Anticipate 3 likely objections\n- [ ] Practice responses out loud\n- [ ] Set time limit (don't negotiate forever)\n\n### During Negotiation\n\n- [ ] Listen 80%, talk 20%\n- [ ] Take notes (shows respect + captures details)\n- [ ] Pause after stating price (silence is power)\n- [ ] Trade, don't concede (every concession = counter-request)\n- [ ] Summarize agreements before ending\n\n### After Negotiation\n\n- [ ] Send summary email within 2 hours\n- [ ] Include agreed scope, price, timeline\n- [ ] Attach updated SOW if changes were made\n- [ ] Set follow-up date\n- [ ] Update CRM with outcome\n\n### Weekly Practice\n\n- [ ] Role-play 1 negotiation scenario with peer\n- [ ] Review 1 lost deal (what objection killed it?)\n- [ ] Refine 1 response based on real feedback\n- [ ] Study 1 negotiation resource (book, podcast, video)\n\n---\n\n> **Next:** [03E: Closing Rituals →](03E-CLOSING-RITUALS.md)\n>\n> *\"The best negotiation is one where both parties feel they got a good deal.\"*\n\n---\n\n*Module 03D | Negotiation Playbook | Version 2026.05*\n"
